From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bron Gondwana <brong@fastmailteam.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 18:11:47 -0800
Subject: [PATCH 1/4] Add a setting to decorate text/plain by wrapping it

Decorating a single-part text/plain message joins the header and footer
to the text itself: the body is decoded, the decorations added, and the
result re-encoded, in whichever of the list's, the message's or utf-8's
charset works.  A utf-8 body usually comes out base64, so every line of
the body changes, and the sender's encoding and octets are gone.  Other
content types are already decorated by wrapping the message in a
multipart/mixed with the header and footer as their own parts.

The new [mailman] decorate_plain_text_in_place setting, 'yes' by default
to keep current behavior, sends text/plain down that wrapping path too
when set to 'no'.  The body then reaches recipients as the sender wrote
it, which matters to anything that records or checks what a list changed,
such as DKIM2 Message-Instance Recipes: the list's change becomes "added
a part before and after" rather than "rewrote every line".  It costs no
extra space or time; it skips the re-encode.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
